BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2026-10-09-computational-phenotypes@edm-megalab.emory.edu
DTSTAMP:20261020T120000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260925T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260925T133000
SUMMARY:Computational phenotypes: traits\, states\, or moving targets? — 
 Emory Decision-Making Megalab
DESCRIPTION:Article discussion. At our September 25 meeting\, we discusse
 d individual differences\, repeated measurement\, parameter stability\, 
 and what makes a useful computational phenotype\, drawing on Schurr et a
 l. (2024) and the companion paper by Katahira\, Oba & Toyama (2024). Fea
 tured article: Dynamic computational phenotyping of human cognition. Aut
 hors: Schurr\, Reznik\, Hillman\, Bhui\, et al.. Journal: Nature Human B
 ehaviour (2024). https://doi.org/10.1038/s41562-024-01814-x Featured art
 icle: Does the reliability of computational models truly improve with hi
 erarchical modeling? Some recommendations and considerations for the ass
 essment of model parameter reliability. Authors: Katahira\, Oba & Toyama
 . Journal: Psychonomic Bulletin & Review (2024). https://doi.org/10.3758
 /s13423-024-02490-8 Lead: Michael Hallquist. Location: PAIS 280.
LOCATION:PAIS 280
URL:https://hallquistlab.github.io/edm_megalab/meetings/2026-09-25/
STATUS:TENTATIVE
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2026-09-11-community-kickoff@edm-megalab.emory.edu
DTSTAMP:20261020T120000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261009T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261009T133000
SUMMARY:What should EDM Megalab be? — Emory Decision-Making Megalab
DESCRIPTION:Community planning meeting. Everyone in the Emory community w
 ith an interest in decision-making is welcome. Building on the broad par
 ticipation at our September 25 meeting\, we will get to know one another
 \, map shared interests\, methods\, datasets\, and opportunities\, and s
 hape priorities for the series. Join the conversation whether you attend
 ed last time or are new to EDM Megalab.  Lead: Michael Hallquist. Locati
 on: PAIS 280.
LOCATION:PAIS 280
URL:https://hallquistlab.github.io/edm_megalab/meetings/2026-10-09/
STATUS:TENTATIVE
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2026-10-23-fmri-preprocessing@edm-megalab.emory.edu
DTSTAMP:20261026T120000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261023T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261023T133000
SUMMARY:WIP presentations — Emory Decision-Making Megalab
DESCRIPTION:Works in progress. Works-in-progress presentations from Pete 
 Hitchcock on a decision-science approach to negative self-judgment in de
 pression and Michael Hallquist on free-operant learning\, vigor\, and ps
 ychopathology\, with time for discussion and feedback. Background papers
  for both presentations are linked with this meeting. No advance reading
  is required. Presentations: Pete Hitchcock: Making Progress on Inherent
 ly Complex ("Low Essentiality") Disorders: A Case Study. Hitchcock intro
 duces the idea that mental health problems vary in their inherent comple
 xity and argues that depression and generalized anxiety are inherently c
 omplex\, or low in essentiality. As a case study in making progress on t
 hese problems\, he describes the lab’s decision-science approach to nega
 tive self-judgment in depression. This work builds on sequential-samplin
 g models\, in which decisions unfold by accumulating evidence over time:
  visual evidence for perceptual judgments\, such as identifying a traffi
 c light’s color\, and personal value associations for food choices\, suc
 h as choosing between Butterfingers and Reese’s.\; Michael Hallquist: Fr
 ee-operant learning\, vigor\, and psychopathology. Optional background: 
 Decision Making and Sequential Sampling from Memory. Authors: Shadlen & 
 Shohamy. Journal: Neuron (2016). Shared by Pete Hitchcock: proposes that
  value-based choices accumulate evidence retrieved from memory\, extendi
 ng sequential-sampling accounts of perceptual decisions. https://pmc.ncb
 i.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4891701/ Optional background: The hippocampus 
 supports deliberation during value-based decisions. Authors: Bakkour\, P
 alombo\, Zylberberg\, Kang\, Reid\, Verfaellie\, Shadlen & Shohamy. Jour
 nal: eLife (2019). Shared by Pete Hitchcock: fMRI and evidence from pati
 ents with hippocampal damage support a role for the hippocampus in delib
 eration during value-based choices. https://elifesciences.org/articles/4
 6080 Background reading: Tonic dopamine: Opportunity costs and the contr
 ol of response vigor. Authors: Niv\, Daw\, Joel & Dayan. Journal: Psycho
 pharmacology (2007). Background on average reward rate\, the opportunity
  cost of time\, and response vigor. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1703
 1711/ Background reading: On two types of deviation from the matching la
 w: Bias and undermatching. Authors: Baum. Journal: Journal of the Experi
 mental Analysis of Behavior (1974). Background on how behavior is alloca
 ted across competing sources of reinforcement. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.
 gov/articles/PMC1333261/ Optional background: A comparison of variable-r
 atio and variable-interval schedules of reinforcement. Authors: Zuriff. 
 Journal: Journal of the Experimental Analysis of Behavior (1970). Shows 
 how different reinforcement contingencies can produce different response
  rates even at equal reinforcement rates. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/a
 rticles/PMC1333678/ Lead: Pete Hitchcock and Michael Hallquist. Location
 : PAIS 280.
LOCATION:PAIS 280
URL:https://hallquistlab.github.io/edm_megalab/meetings/2026-10-23/
STATUS:TENTATIVE
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
